  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1134 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nMid level high center will dominate the extended period. There\nremains plenty of uncertainty regarding it exact location after\nWed when agreement is good it will position across WRN OK and the\nERN Panhandles. A subtle decrease in H5 heights was noted the last\nfew operational model runs and NBM actually decreased temps a\ncouple degrees the last run or two, but is still showing plenty of\n100-107 degrees highs during the peak Wed through Fri. NBM\nprobability of >104 degree readings is anywhere from 35 to 85 \npercent across the N/CEN Panhandles, across the central Canadian \nRiver Valley and from PDC to Wellington on Thu. Probabilities are\nsimilar through the weekend, but briefly shift a little south (focused\nmore on the CEN and SRN Texas Panhandle) for Friday. There is a \n20-40 percent chance for readings exceeding 109, but this is \nconfined to the typically hottest areas near Borger and PDC \nThursday and into the weekend. There is some uncertainty regarding\nexact location of two frontal/outflow boundaries that will stall \nvery close to the NE Panhandles Wed and again Fri and the \nnortheast is where the lowest confidence is as a result. The\ncombination of forecast temperatures (highs and lows) and\nlongevity of the event is leading to a large area of forecasted \nextreme heat risk (via the NWS HeatRisk product) by late week and\ncontinuing through the weekend. Models do not agree on the \nposition of the ridge beyond mid week. Some retrograde it west of \nthe area and open the door for NW flow S/WVs to induce storms as \nthere is a significantly high theta-e axis stretched across the \narea, although even in those models the mid level temps are very \nwarm. This is leading to a low POP Friday and through the weekend \nwith best chances in the NW Panhandles. Ridge appears to dominate \nenough to promote continued 100+ degree heat through the weekend.",
